    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `TelephoneE164.java`.\n\n**Change:** After the existing generic normalization (strip separators, apply/keep country code, enforce the overall 8\u201315 digit E.164 range), the `normalize` method now validates the **national-number length against the country code**:\n\n- Added a longest-prefix-first `NATIONAL_LENGTHS` map: `\"61\" \u2192 9`, `\"1\" \u2192 10`.\n- After confirming the digits are valid, it finds the matching country-code prefix and checks the remaining (national) digit count. If the national length is wrong for that country, `normalize` returns `null`.\n\nReturning `null` flows through the existing path: `ValidateOwner` throws `InvalidTelephoneException`, which `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler` turns into a **400**. So `\"+61 123\"` (only 3 national digits, needs 9) is rejected, while a valid `+61` number with 9 national digits or a `+1` number with 10 is accepted.\n\nThe map is ordered longest-prefix-first (`61` checked before `1`) so a `+61` number isn't mistaken for a `+1` one. Both the targeted acceptance test (`errorRejectsWrongNationalLength`) and the full suite are green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
